# 19. CRITICAL DEVELOPMENT RULES

Follow these rules throughout the entire project.

### Scientific Accuracy

- Never fabricate NASA data.
- Never fabricate p-values.
- Never fabricate statistical significance.
- Never invent dataset coverage.
- Never misrepresent model outputs as direct observations.
- Never confuse temperature anomalies with absolute temperatures.
- Never infer local climate trends from global averages.
- Never claim a relationship is causal based only on correlation.
- Always display units.
- Always document uncertainty.
- Always preserve source attribution.

### Engineering Quality

- Use clean architecture.
- Use reusable components.
- Use strict TypeScript.
- Use Python type hints.
- Validate inputs.
- Handle errors properly.
- Avoid duplicated business logic.
- Avoid hardcoded scientific results.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies.
- Write automated tests.
- Keep frontend and backend responsibilities separate.

### User Experience

- Do not create dead buttons.
- Do not use placeholder charts that appear to represent real NASA results.
- Clearly distinguish loading, unavailable and insufficient-data states.
- Show users where the data comes from.
- Explain technical terminology in understandable language.
- Make the product responsive.

### Development Workflow

- Complete phases sequentially.
- Run tests after every major implementation.
- Fix errors before moving forward.
- Maintain project documentation.
- Update the README as the project evolves.
- Record architectural decisions.
- Do not abandon existing working features.
- Do not report tasks as completed unless they have actually been verified.

